rn markers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a47bf94d/D2/3/code.py:280:    - A color-5 cell is not a generic crossing bracket anymore: its left/right placement beside a crossing tells which horizontal arm is connected to the vertical stem there.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a47bf94d/D2/3/code.py:285:    - While tracing the wire, an unmarked crossing goes straight, a left-side marker joins the west arm, and a right-side marker joins the east arm; color-9 rectangles still act as wide pass-through blocks.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a47bf94d/D2/3/code.py:288:    - The token-shape logic is unchanged; the new difficulty is that wire routing now depends on marker side, so you must read \"which side is marked\" rather than just \"is there a crossing marker\".\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a47bf94d/D2/3/code.py:368:# --------------- generator (single_sided_cross_turn) ---------------\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e8686506/D8/3/code.py:48:#    Across examples, the input visibly has one large symmetric frame plus a handful of\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D13/2/code.py:15:#   - In the output, each active trigger becomes a 3x3 diagonal cross/X:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D13/2/code.py:17:#   - The two arms aligned with the segment visually interrupt the blue diagonal at the crossing.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D13/2/code.py:20:#   - Example: for a \"\\\" blue segment crossing a trigger at (4, 4), the original task emitted \"/\" rays from cells adjacent to (4, 4). The variant paints the X centered at (4, 4), so the \"/\" rays now continue outward from (3, 5) and (5, 3), beginning their new cells at (2, 6) and (6, 2).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D13/2/code.py:42:def _cross_cells(r: int, c: int) -> set[tuple[int, int]]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db695cfb/D13/2/code.py:55:    Connect same-color diagonal pixel pairs, then turn crossed trigger pixels into X-marks that launch perpendicul