eshi/code/269e22fb/D12/2/code.py:16:#      solver decodes (r,c) from the marker first and then tests only that offset across orientations/polarities.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/269e22fb/D12/2/code.py:33:#    - Across examples, the crop can look locally similar while the marker moves to different board positions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/269e22fb/D12/2/code.py:222:    - Decode the crop's top-left offset from the marker position, then test only that offset across motif orientations and both foreground/background polarities.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D2/2/code.py:32:#       Visually this is a cross inside the right chamber. Because the horizontal left+right\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D2/2/code.py:38:#       - Original output paints rows 5,6,7 red across cols sep+1..w-1.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D2/2/code.py:41:#       - In addition, all three crosses share the same launch column sep+2 (or w-1 when\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D2/2/code.py:49:#     - The right chamber keeps width 2-4, so the cross never becomes visually noisy.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D2/2/code.py:132:def _paint_cross_rays(out: np.ndarray, sep: int, trigger_rows: list[int], bg: int, red: int) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D2/2/code.py:150:    Slide black shapes rightward to a grey separator, then emit four-direction crosses from enclosed holes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D2/2/code.py:159:    - For every row touched by such a hole, emit a red cross into the right chamber: the horizontal arm spans the whole right-side row, and a shared vertical arm rises and falls through the launch column.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D2/2/code.py:161:    Key insight: The enclosing hole test is unchanged from the original task; the variant only changes the ray path after activation, turning each row-wise outlet into a cross-shaped projection in the right chamber.